WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:08.952
Dogsledding—it's an exciting sport, but lots of hard work!

2
00:00:09.692 --> 00:00:11.493
That's Kerry's team over there.

3
00:00:12.113 --> 00:00:13.894
They are ready to start the race.

4
00:00:14.674 --> 00:00:16.615
Haze is the leader of the pack.

5
00:00:17.235 --> 00:00:18.936
He is an Alaskan husky.

6
00:00:20.417 --> 00:00:22.778
Any type of dog can be a sled dog.

7
00:00:23.478 --> 00:00:27.200
Okay, almost any type; some are too small!

8
00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:34.603
Some popular sled dogs are Alaskan malamutes, Siberian huskies, and Eskimo dogs.

9
00:00:37.444 --> 00:00:39.625
Sled dogs must be very strong.

10
00:00:40.265 --> 00:00:42.386
For example, look at Carmen.

11
00:00:43.087 --> 00:00:47.369
She can pull over 350 kilograms by herself!

12
00:00:47.389 --> 00:00:50.710
"Okay, guys!

13
00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:52.271
Are you ready to run?"

14
00:00:52.931 --> 00:00:53.871
That's Kerry.

15
00:00:54.211 --> 00:00:55.072
He's the musher.

16
00:00:55.836 --> 00:00:58.117
The musher is the driver of the sled.

17
00:00:58.737 --> 00:01:03.098
It's very important for the musher and the pack to trust each other.

18
00:01:05.019 --> 00:01:09.420
Mushers usually don't have trouble getting the dogs to pull the sled.

19
00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:14.101
Most of the time, it's hard to get the dogs to stop pulling!

20
00:01:15.601 --> 00:01:19.422
Before a competition, the dogs train for months.

21
00:01:20.043 --> 00:01:23.884
Kerry trains his team by having them pull his car!

22
00:01:23.904 --> 00:01:25.224
"Come on, team!

23
00:01:25.728 --> 00:01:26.648
Faster, Haze!"

24
00:01:29.969 --> 00:01:34.250
Since sled dogs train so hard, they need lots of food.

25
00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:40.232
A normal dog usually eats about 1,500 calories a day, or less.

26
00:01:40.832 --> 00:01:44.653
But sled dogs eat about ten thousand calories a day!

27
00:01:45.413 --> 00:01:46.994
That's a lot of food!

28
00:01:50.034 --> 00:01:53.735
Although sled dogs are tough, they have sensitive feet.

29
00:01:54.336 --> 00:01:57.600
Kerry, the musher, ties booties on their feet.

30
00:01:58.361 --> 00:02:01.842
These booties protect the paws and keep them warm.

31
00:02:04.642 --> 00:02:06.943
Each dogsled competition is different.

32
00:02:07.623 --> 00:02:13.365
Some teams have 16 dogs, while bigger ones have more than 200 dogs!

33
00:02:14.045 --> 00:02:17.106
Some races are short, while others are long.

34
00:02:17.766 --> 00:02:19.746
Some races last for days.

35
00:02:22.567 --> 00:02:25.308
Sled dogs like cold, snowy days.

36
00:02:26.064 --> 00:02:29.125
They run faster and longer when there is snow.

37
00:02:29.666 --> 00:02:31.667
The snow is soft on their feet.

38
00:02:32.527 --> 00:02:36.169
And the cold weather keeps the dogs from getting too hot!

39
00:02:38.149 --> 00:02:41.651
The mushers, like Kerry, must be strong leaders.

40
00:02:42.511 --> 00:02:46.713
If the musher gets tired or uncertain, the dogs sense it.

41
00:02:47.393 --> 00:02:50.255
Then the dogs begin to distrust the musher.

42
00:02:50.955 --> 00:02:52.916
That could be very dangerous.

43
00:02:54.768 --> 00:02:58.151
Winning a dogsled competition is very exciting.

44
00:02:58.692 --> 00:03:00.494
But it's not all about winning.

45
00:03:01.154 --> 00:03:02.355
It's about teamwork.

46
00:03:02.876 --> 00:03:03.957
It's about trust.

47
00:03:04.638 --> 00:03:06.660
Dogsledding is about friendship.

48
00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:09.502
The best kind of friendship there is.
